WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:04.630
In the red corner, weighing in at 36 solar
masses, the reigning champion,

00:00:04.630 --> 00:00:07.230
Big brother black hole clone!

00:00:07.230 --> 00:00:11.520
In the blue corner, weighing in at 29 solar
masses, the challenger,

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:14.180
Little brother black hole clone!

00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:15.700
It’s going to be an epic fight.

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:16.660
How will it end?

00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:18.540
Let’s find out with some physics!

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:21.820
Hey Crazies.

00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:25.720
Some of you were wondering if I was ever going
to do a video on Gravitational Waves.

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:27.100
Well now’s the time!

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:30.360
I realize the hype is over and there are already
tons of videos about this,

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:32.520
but there are still some connections that
need to be made

00:00:32.529 --> 00:00:35.340
and some very misleading visuals that need
to be addressed.

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:38.329
Plus it doesn’t hurt to get your information
from multiple sources.

00:00:38.329 --> 00:00:40.380
Especially with something this weird and crazy.

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:42.340
So let’s get back to those black-hole clones!

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:46.640
As the black-hole clones circle each other,
they move closer and closer and speed up in

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:47.580
the process.

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:51.140
Eventually, there’s a connection, does one
go down for the count?

00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:54.300
No, don’t be ridiculous! This is astrophysics.

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:57.040
The two combine into one giant black-hole
clone.

00:00:57.050 --> 00:00:58.770
They’re like a cosmic Voltron!

00:00:58.770 --> 00:00:59.580
Voltron.

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:00.920
Defender of the Universe?

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:02.400
Very successful cartoon?

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:03.600
Someone has to know Voltron.

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:04.780
I’m so old.

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:08.860
Anyway, the combined giant black-hole clone
is 62 solar masses,

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:10.040
which might leave you thinking:

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:14.100
Isn’t 36 + 29 = 65, not 62?

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:15.960
And why weren’t their orbits stable?

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:18.940
Both very good questions and both have the
same answer:

00:01:18.940 --> 00:01:20.800
Gravitational waves!

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:25.000
See, waves are a big deal because they move
energy from one place to another

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:27.830
without having to move matter from one place
to another.

00:01:27.830 --> 00:01:30.240
That’s the definition of a wave.

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:34.660
They’re so useful that not 1 but 2 of our
senses have evolved to detect them.

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:39.840
Mechanical Waves are waves in matter
like strings, air, the Earth, or water.

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:42.880
Those are the kind you think of when I say
the word "wave."

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:47.520
Light is also a wave, but in electric and
magnetic fields instead of matter.

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:49.540
We did a whole video on light if you’re
interested!

00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:51.860
Since there isn’t a lot of matter in space,

00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:55.540
light was the only way we were able to find
astronomical stuff.

00:01:55.540 --> 00:01:58.800
Stars like the Sun emit visible light, so
we can see them,

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:02.480
but planets like Jupiter don’t, so we have
to wait for light from the Sun

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:06.660
to get there, bounce off, and then come to
Earth before we can see it.

00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:09.920
Unfortunately, not everything can emit or
reflect enough light.

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:11.980
Black Holes and Neutron Stars, for example.

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:15.540
Sometimes we get lucky and those things pull
in matter that we can see,

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:17.240
but otherwise we’re left in the dark.

00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:19.920
That’s why gravitational waves are such
a big deal.

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:21.920
They’re a whole new type of wave.

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:26.200
Wiggles in the fabric of space-time itself,
but that means they act a little differently

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:28.600
than other types of waves,
so let’s be careful.

00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:32.780
For something one-dimensional like this string,
you only need one number to represent its

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:33.480
curvature.

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:37.720
For curvature on a two-dimensional surface,
you also only need one number,

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:40.600
but the same cannot be said for higher dimensions.

00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:43.720
So this is a terrible graphic for space-time.

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:47.860
For curvature within of a three-dimensional
space, you need 6 numbers.

00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:51.920
For curvature within a four-dimensional space-time
like our universe,

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:56.500
you need 20 numbers,
which results in 10 independent Einstein equations.

00:02:56.500 --> 00:03:00.820
Those equations describe all of the curvature
that results in gravitational motion.

00:03:00.820 --> 00:03:03.060
But not all curvature creates waves.

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:06.500
You can’t have anything that’s spherically
or cylindrically symmetric,

00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:10.300
so a lonely spinning black hole probably isn’t
going to do it,

00:03:10.300 --> 00:03:15.260
but two black holes orbiting each other will,
just like our black-hole clones from early.

00:03:15.260 --> 00:03:19.220
And that’s exactly what LIGO detected on
September 14th, 2015.

00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:20.050
What’s LIGO?

00:03:20.050 --> 00:03:23.440
The coolest thing to happen in experimental
physics since the LHC!

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:28.030
LIGO stands for Laser Interferometer Gravitational-wave
Observatory.

00:03:28.030 --> 00:03:31.340
They detect gravitational waves using lasers.

00:03:31.340 --> 00:03:37.380
About 1.3 billion years ago, a 36 solar-mass
black-hole and a 29 solar-mass black-hole

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:43.460
collided to form a 62 solar-mass black-hole,
releasing gravitational waves carrying 3 solar-masses

00:03:43.460 --> 00:03:46.940
worth of energy
and then those waves traveled 1.3 billion

00:03:46.940 --> 00:03:48.240
light years to get to Earth.

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:49.820
How do we know all of that?

00:03:49.820 --> 00:03:51.420
OK, I guess that’s a fair question.

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:55.220
The easiest information to get from a wave
is frequency and energy.

00:03:55.220 --> 00:03:57.180
But that’s plenty if you can do some physics.

00:03:57.180 --> 00:04:01.220
The frequency of the wave is twice the orbital
speed of black holes.

00:04:01.220 --> 00:04:05.380
Using general relativity, orbital speed gives
us the masses of the black holes.

00:04:05.380 --> 00:04:09.220
and those masses tell us the energy they’re
going to lose during the merger.

00:04:09.220 --> 00:04:14.260
That energy spreads thinner the farther it
travels, until it finally arrives at LIGO.

00:04:14.260 --> 00:04:18.300
Comparing the predicted energy to the energy
LIGO actually received

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:20.100
gives us the distance to the black holes.

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:22.120
Direction is a little harder.

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:24.220
We don’t aim LIGO like a telescope.

00:04:24.220 --> 00:04:28.360
It just sits there on the surface of the Earth
and monitors all directions at once,

00:04:28.360 --> 00:04:31.440
which is partly why we made two different
observatories.

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:34.560
One is in Louisiana and the other is in Washington
state.

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:39.040
Louisiana detected the event a whole 7 milliseconds
before Washington.

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:42.260
And since we know gravitational waves travel
at the speed of light,

00:04:42.260 --> 00:04:43.400
we can get a direction.

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:44.940
Well, roughly anyway.

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:48.960
LIGO could only narrow down the location of
the merger to an area of the sky,

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:53.700
measuring 600 square degrees, which is a little
under 1% of the sky.

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:55.760
But that’s pretty impressive considering
it monitors

00:04:55.760 --> 00:05:00.740
out to 5 billion light years using 2.5 mi
long vacuum tubes

00:05:00.740 --> 00:05:05.240
containing lasers that change in length by
a fraction of the width of a proton.

00:05:05.240 --> 00:05:09.140
And since LIGO showed proof of concept, other
countries are now building their own,

00:05:09.140 --> 00:05:11.600
which is going to make our measurements much more
precise.

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:13.180
It’s a great time to be alive!

00:05:13.180 --> 00:05:14.840
Thanks for liking and sharing this video.

00:05:14.840 --> 00:05:17.700
Don’t forget to subscribe if you’d like
to see more science like this.

00:05:17.700 --> 00:05:21.220
And until next time, remember, it’s OK to
be a little crazy.

